Abstract
AbstractMembrane filtration is an effective method for eliminating contaminants from water, but its performance is restricted by the trade-off between permeability and selectivity, as well as the serious membrane fouling. In this work, a novel porous carbon membrane (PCM) was constructed by coating porous carbon derived from metal-organic frameworks on ceramic membrane support. The PCM possessed good electrical conductivity, large surface area and hierarchical porous structure, making it promising to couple membrane filtration with electrochemistry for improved water treatment. Under electrochemical assistance, the PCM displayed enhanced selectivity and antifouling capability towards the water treatment. At -1.5 V, the PCM showed enhanced removal and reduced flux loss for the removal of suspended particles with size comparable to membrane pore size. Moreover, the PCM under +1.5 V exhibited effective removal of the organic chemicals unfavorable for size-exclusion by the membrane, whose removal efficiencies towards phenol and methyl orange were 3.0 and 3.3 times higher than those without voltage supply, respectively. The PCM with electrochemical assistance also displayed superior performance than PCM alone in real water treatment. The outstanding performance of the PCM under electrochemical assistance was mainly owing to its large electro-active surface area and enhanced mass transfer rate.
Keywords: Membrane filtration; porous carbon; electrochemical assistance; water treatment; antifouling
## 1. Introduction
The deteriorated water environment quality caused by the pollutant discharge from industry, agriculture and human activity has received increasing concern [1, 2]. Especially, some persistent organic contaminants, which may induce irreversible detriment to ecological environment and human health, are difficult to be adequately addressed by conventional water treatment technology [3]. Microfiltration or ultrafiltration based technology has been widely applied for water treatment due to its easy operation, no chemical addition, low energy consumption and high productivity [4, 5]. However, it is unavailable for microfiltration or ultrafiltration membrane to separate the organic pollutants with sizes much smaller than membrane pores, while some of the them could pose a serious threat on the ecological system even at trace concentrations due to their high toxicity and bioaccumulation [6]. Moreover, the membrane fouling is inevitable during the membrane filtration process, giving rise to the performance decay and increase of energy consumption [7-9]. Therefore, it is necessary to develop novel membranes with high water flux, high selectivity, and also good antifouling capability.
Recently, carbon nanomaterials such as reduced graphene oxide (rGO) and carbon nanotube (CNT) have exhibited great potential in constructing separation membrane with both high selectivity and permeability, owing to their environmental friendly nature, tunable physicochemical properties, ultrafast water transport and excellent sieving capabilities [10-12]. In addition, the good electrical conductivity of these carbon materials renders these carbon-based membranes with electrochemical function for further improving the membrane performance [13-15]. The electrochemical behaviors, such as electrically-enhanced adsorption, electrostatic repulsion and electrochemical oxidation, are beneficial for the removal of pollutants with size smaller than membrane pores and membrane fouling inhibition, thus further enhancing the selectivity and permeability of carbon-based membrane [10, 16, 17]. Despite the above progress, the low specific surface area and nonporous structure of current rGO or CNT based membranes limit the mass transfer rate and the accessible electro-active sites for
pollutants removal, which are regarded as the critical factors influencing the efficiency in electrochemical membrane filtration process [17, 18]. Hence, developing novel carbon membranes with high performance is still highly desirable but remains challenging.
In the past decades, there is a rapid development of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derived porous carbons [19-21]. It is a facile and scalable route to construct porous carbons through direct carbonization of MOFs [22, 23]. The nanoporous carbons derived from MOFs show large surface area and hierarchically porous structure [24, 25], which are favorable for constructing novel membranes with improved selectivity and permeability. More importantly, the carbonized porous carbons also possess good electrical conductivity and are widely employed in electrochemical process [26, 27]. Recently, the porous carbons derived from MOFs are found to be highly efficient in electrocatalysis due to their plentiful exposed electroactive sites, shortened diffusion pathway for reactant transfer and facile surface chemistry regulation, which are regarded as important factors in electrochemical process [28-31]. Benefiting from these merits, the porous carbon membrane derived from MOFs may serve as an ideal platform to integrate membrane filtration and electrochemical function for enhanced water treatment.
In this work, MOF-derived porous carbon membrane (PCM) was firstly constructed by coating porous carbons on ceramic membrane substrate through a vacuum filtration-pyrolysis method. MOF-5 was chosen as the precursors of porous carbon due to its facile synthesis, high specific surface area and high pore volume [32, 33]. When an external voltage was applied on the electrical conductive porous carbon layer, the PCM was endowed with electrochemical functions (e.g. electrically enhanced adsorption, electrostatic repulsion and electro-oxidation) beneficial for pollutant removal. The ubiquitous refractory pollutants, such as phenol and methyl orange, were used to evaluate the filtration performance of PCM under electrochemical assistance. The performance of electrochemically assisted PCM for surface water treatment was also investigated. Based on the above results, the filtration mechanism of PCM under electrochemical assistance was discussed.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals, including 2-amino terephthalic acid (99%), \( \mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) (99%), \( N, N \)-dimethylformamide (DMF) and other chemicals were obtained from Guangfu Fine Chemicals, Tianjin, China. Polyacrylonitrile (PAN) was obtained from Sigma-Aldrich, St. Louis). They were all of analytical grade, which were used with no further purification. The solutions were prepared using ultrapure water (electrical resistivity \( >18\,\mathrm{M}\Omega \cdot \mathrm{cm} \), Laike Instrument Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China).
## 2.2. Preparation of MOF-derived porous carbon
In a typical procedure for MOF-5 synthesis, \( 0.27\,\mathrm{g} \) terephthalic acid and \( 1.43\,\mathrm{g} \) \( \mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) were dissolved in \( N, N \)-dimethylformamide (DMF, \( 60\,\mathrm{mL} \)). The resulting homogeneous solution was then transferred to a \( 80\,\mathrm{mL} \) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and maintained at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \). Colorless MOF-5 crystals were obtained after pouring the mother solution. The crystals were rinsed with DMF and activated in acetone. Subsequently, the synthesized MOF-5 was transferred to a tube furnace for carbonization, which was heated with a rising rate of \( 10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1} \) conducted at \( 1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) under Ar atmosphere for \( 3\,\mathrm{h} \).
## 2.3. Preparation of porous carbon membrane (PCM)
The synthetic procedure of ceramic membrane substrate was in accordance with the methods reported previously [34, 35]. In a typical synthetic process of PCM, a vacuum filtration-thermal treatment method was used to coat the porous carbon layer on porous \( \mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) substrate. First, the porous carbon was dispersed in DMF \( (0.5\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}) \) with different ratio of PAN. Then, the mixtures were vacuum-filtered onto a porous \( \mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) membrane substrate. The resulting porous carbon membrane was heated at \( 250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 3\,\mathrm{h} \) in air followed by pyrolyzed at \( 1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) in Ar condition. In order to achieve the best membrane performance, the PAN ratio (mass ratio of PAN in carbon/PAN mixture) and loading of porous carbon/PAN mixture were varied to adjust the membrane mechanical strength, electrical conductivity, pore size, as well as the permeate flux of membrane.
## 2.4. Characterization
2.4. CharacterizationThe morphologies of porous carbon and membrane were observed on the Hitachi S-4800 scanning electron microscope (SEM). The structure characteristics of the membranes were analyzed with X-ray diffraction (Shimadzu LabXXRD-6000, XRD) and Raman spectrum (Renishaw Micro-Raman system 2000 spectrometer). To exclude the influence of ceramic membrane substrate, the porous carbon powders were scraped from membrane for measurement. The BET surface area and pore structure of porous carbon were examined via \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherms with a Quadrasorb instrument. The electrical conductivity of PCM was tested using the four-probe measurements (keithley 2400, USA). The pore size distribution of PCMs was analyzed by a Capillary Flow Porometer (Porolux 1000, IB-FTGmbH). Cyclic voltammetry (CV) test was carried out in an electrochemical workstation (CHI660D, Shanghai Chenhua Ltd., China). The PCM, saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and Pt sheet were used as the working electrode, reference electrode and the counter electrode, respectively, and the \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution containing phenol or methyl orange was used as the electrolyte. To test the electro-active surface area and mass transfer rate constant of PCM, rGO membrane and CNT membrane, the CV scan and linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) scans were performed using carbon material (porous carbon, rGO and CNT) coated ceramic membrane as the working electrode (prepared in the same procedure as the PCM), SCE as the reference electrode and the Pt sheet as the counter electrode. The fabrication of CNT membrane and rGO membrane follows the same method as the PCM. The CV curve was conducted from -0.5 to \(1.5\,\mathrm{V}\) in the electrolyte of \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\), while the LSV curve was carried out from 0.5 to \(2.5\,\mathrm{V}\) in the electrolyte of \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{KH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) and \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{K}_{4}\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\).
## 2.5. The membrane performance tests
2.5. The membrane performance testsThe filtration experiments were performed in a dead-end manner (Fig. S1 and S2). A plate membrane with an area of \(8.04\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) was used in all experiments. During the electrochemically assisted membrane filtration process, the PCM served as the anode while the Ti mesh acted as the cathode. A DC power supply (WYJS-60V/2A, Shanghai
Shanjie electrical technology, co., Ltd., China) was used to provide the external voltage. The distance between the PCM and titanium mesh is \(1\;\mathrm{cm}\).
In the water treatment experiments, polystyrene (PS) microspheres \((30\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}})\) with a size of \(200\;\mathrm{nm}\) were used to examine the performance of PCM for the removal of suspended particles in water under the electrochemical assistance. The filtrate samples collected at certain internals were employed for further analysis and simultaneously the permeate flux was determined. The concentration of PS microsphere in the permeate water was determined through TOC analyzer (TOC- \(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{CPH}}\), Shimadzu, Japan). During the filtration process, the transmembrane pressure was fixed at \(0.2\;\mathrm{bar}\).
Phenol and methyl orange (MO) with the same concentration of \(10\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\) were used as the model pollutants to evaluate the performance of PCM for the removal of organic chemical under electrochemical assistance. The filtrate samples were obtained at certain internals for measurement of pollutant concentration. The concentration of phenol in the permeate water was measured by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC; Waters, Milford, USA). The concentration of MO were determined using a UV-vis spectrometer at \(\lambda=464\;\mathrm{nm}\). Unless otherwise mentioned, \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) was added in all the tested solution samples as electrolyte.
In real water treatment, the performance of PCM was evaluated in terms of the removal of \(\mathrm{UV}_{254}\), TOC, turbidity and bacteria, as well as the permeate flux.
## 3. Results and Discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of PCMs
The morphologies of porous carbon and PCM were shown in Fig. 1. The porous carbon derived from MOF-5 exhibits a cubic shape with size of \(\sim20\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Fig. 1a). As shown in Fig. 1b, the porous ceramic membrane is composed of ceramic particles and no crack is observed. After deposition of porous carbons, it could be clearly found that the cubic-like porous carbons are stacked on the ceramic membrane (Fig. 1c). The thickness of porous carbon layer is measured to be about \(36\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Fig. 1d).
The Raman spectrum of PCM is displayed in Fig. 2a. The peaks at \(1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1360~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to the G (graphitic carbon) peak and D (disordered carbon) peak of the carbon, respectively, and the relative ratio of D and G peaks, which is denoted as the \(\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}\), is approximately 0.968, indicating the porous carbon layer is mainly composed of graphitic carbon. In addition, the crystal structure of PCM was also characterized and shown in Fig. 2b. The peaks located at \(25^{\circ}\) and \(44^{\circ}\) referred to the characteristic (002) and (100) diffractions of carbon, respectively, in consistent with the result of Raman spectrum. Additionally, no other diffraction peaks was observed, suggesting the Zn metal was evaporated due to the high carbonization temperature \((1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). Owing to main component of graphitic carbon, the electrical conductivity of PCM reached \(1.30\;\mathrm{S\cdot cm}^{-1}\), which is high enough to realize the electrochemical function on membrane.
Table S1 displays the pore volume and specific surface area of porous carbons derived from MOF-5. The porous carbon inherited the high BET surface area and porous structure from MOF-5, which shows a large specific surface area of \(754\,\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), high micropore volume of \(0.41\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and total pore volume of \(1.21\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The PCM displaying the above features is beneficial for mass diffusion, reactants adsorption, and water transportation, which may realize the simultaneous improvement of the selectivity and permeability of membrane.
## 3.2. Effect of PAN ratio and porous carbon loading on membrane performance
It has been reported that the adhesion force between CNTs and porous support could be enhanced by graphene-like carbon produced from pyrolysis of PAN [10]. Hence, in this study, the ratio of PAN (the mass ratio of PAN in the CNT/PAN mixture) was tuned to investigate its effect on membrane mechanical strength. The mechanical strength of membranes with different PAN ratio was examined through ultrasonic treatment of these membranes for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). Evidently, serious loss of porous carbon was observed on membrane with low PAN ratio, but this circumstance is largely improved by increasing the PAN ratio (Fig. S3). It confirms the PAN is able to enhance the mechanical strength of PCM. Moreover, it was found the PAN ratio could also influence the membrane permeability. As shown in the Fig. S4, with the increase of PAN proportion from \(0.05\%\) to \(0.1\%\), the pure water flux decreased slightly from 192 to \(118\,\mathrm{L{\cdot}m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{bar}^{-1}\).
However, when the PAN proportion was further increased from \(0.1\%\) to \(0.4\%\), the pure water flux increased significantly from 118 to \(824\ \mathrm{L\cdot m^{-2}\cdot h^{-1}\cdot bar^{-1}}\). This unexpected phenomenon might be attributed to the formed nitrogen doped carbon after the pyrolysis of PAN, for which N doping is beneficial for enhancing the hydrophilicity of membrane surface [36]. To obtain the membrane with good mechanical strength and high permeate flux, the PAN ratio of \(0.4\%\) was chosen for preparing PCM.
Since the porous carbon serves as the separation layer, its loading is another factor which could influence the sieving and permeability property of membrane. In this work, different porous carbon loadings (0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0 and \(2.5\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\)) were selected. The data of PCM loading of \(0.5\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\) was not shown because the membrane integrity was poor at such low loading. The pore size distribution and pure water flux of ceramic substrate are shown in Fig. S5. Table S2 shows the electrical conductivity of PCM at different loadings of porous carbon. It could be found that the electrical conductivity was negligible at low loading of \(1.0\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\) (only \(0.57\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\)). As the loading increased from 1.5 to \(2.0\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\), the electrical conductivity increased dramatically and reached \(1.30\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\), which was high enough to achieve its electrochemical function. Notably, when the loading was further increased from 2.0 to \(2.5\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\), the electrical conductivity showed slight increase from 1.30 to \(1.34\ \mathrm{S\cdot cm^{-1}}\), meaning higher porous carbon loading contributed little to the electrical conductivity. Fig. 3a shows that when the loading of porous carbon varied from 1.0 to \(2.0\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\), the pore size distribution decreased gradually with the mean pore size dropping from \(520\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(280\ \mathrm{nm}\), and the pure water flux decreased gradually from 1440 to \(824\ \mathrm{L\cdot m^{-2}\cdot h^{-1}\cdot bar^{-1}}\) (Fig. 3b). As the loading was further increased to \(2.5\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\), a slight decrease of permeate flux from 824 to \(720\ \mathrm{L\cdot m^{-2}\cdot h^{-1}\cdot bar^{-1}}\) was observed, despite the obvious decrease of mean pore size from \(280\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(210\ \mathrm{nm}\). This phenomenon may be attributed to the deposition of more hydrophilic pyrolytic PAN on membrane with the increasing loading, which mitigates the flux decline. Combining the results of electrical conductivity, pore size distribution and pure water flux, the \(2.0\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\) was chosen as the catalyst loading.
The pore size distribution of PCM with \(2\ \mathrm{mg\cdot cm^{-2}}\) loading suggests it is a microfiltration membrane (280 nm). Moreover, the membrane can withstand a wide
range of pressure (0.5-2.0 bar), suggesting its good mechanical strength. Compared with the commercial ceramic membrane with the comparable pore size (300 nm), the PCM with an additional thick separation layer of 36 µm shows 1.13 times higher permeability (Table S3). The high permeability of PCM is possibly attributed to its hierarchical porous structure favorable for water transport and hydrophilic carbon surface.
3.3. Performance of PCM for polystyrene microspheres removal under electrochemical assistance
Suspended particles are widely presented in the natural water and serve as a main source for membrane fouling during the membrane treatment process. To simulate the particulate matter in water, the PS microspheres with a diameter of 200 nm, which is comparable to the average pore size of membrane (280 nm), were used to assess the rejection and antifouling property of PCM under electrochemical assistance. Fig. 4a shows that the membrane filtration alone could remove 75% PS microsphere mainly via size-exclusion after reaching equilibrium. When an external voltage of +1.0 or -1.0 V was applied on membrane, the removal efficiency was enhanced to 82% or 84%, demonstrating the voltage supply, regardless of negative or positive, is beneficial for enhancing the rejection efficiency of PCM. Moreover, the antifouling capability of
PCM under electrochemical assistance was also examined. Fig. 4b shows that the flux loss of PCM alone was significant in the absence of voltage supply (45%, the ratio of decreased permeate flux to pure water flux) after reaching equilibrium, which was possibly owing to the pore blockage or filtration cake formation induced by the retained PS microsphere. Evidently, the flux loss of PCM under \(+1.0\ \mathrm{V}\) increased to 60%, whereas it decreased to 25% for PCM under \(-1.0\ \mathrm{V}\). To elucidate the above interesting phenomena, the Zeta potential of the PS microsphere was determined. The result showed that the PS microsphere was negatively charged with a Zeta potential of \(-28.3\ \mathrm{mV}\). It suggests that the enhanced removal of PS microsphere under \(-1.0\ \mathrm{V}\) is attributed to the electrostatic repulsion between PS microsphere and membrane, and this process could mitigate the membrane fouling due to the less access of PS microsphere to membrane surface. On the other hand, the \(+1.0\ \mathrm{V}\) could also enhance the removal of PS microsphere via electrostatic attraction, but the accumulation of PS microsphere on membrane lowered the permeate flux of membrane. To support the above results, the SEM images of the treated PCM with and without voltage supply are shown in Fig. S6. At \(+1.0\ \mathrm{V}\), it is obvious the membrane surface was seriously fouled by PS microsphere. In contrast to that, less PS microsphere accumulates on the surface of PCM at \(-1.0\ \mathrm{V}\). The results further confirm that the electrostatic repulsion could indeed reduce the membrane fouling caused by PS microsphere. To achieve both good selectivity and permeability in separating PS microsphere, the negative voltage supply is more favorable. For further investigating the effect of voltage supply to membrane performance, different negative voltage supply varying from \(-0.5\) to \(-1.5\ \mathrm{V}\) were employed. It was found the removal efficiency of PS microsphere gradually increased with the increase of negative voltage from \(-0.5\) to \(-1.5\ \mathrm{V}\). At \(-1.5\ \mathrm{V}\), almost 85% PS microsphere could be removed. On the other hand, the flux loss also decreases from 45% to 20% with the increase of voltage supply from 0 to \(-1.5\ \mathrm{V}\). The results demonstrate that the rejection and antifouling capability of PCM could be enhanced with the increase of negative voltage supply.
3.4. Performance of PCM for organic molecules removal under electrochemical assistance
Organic chemicals, such as the phenol and dyes, pose potential threat to human beings and ecological environment even at trace concentration [37, 38]. However, due to their small size, these organic molecules are difficult to be rejected by traditional microfiltration or ultrafiltration membrane. In this work, the capability of PCM for the removal of organic molecules was evaluated under electrochemical assistance. Fig 5a shows that the PCM alone could remove 25% phenol within the 60 min operation. As is known, the size of phenol (0.5 nm) is much lower than the pore size of PCM (280 nm). The phenol removal was mainly attributed to the high adsorption capability of porous carbon layer due to its large surface area. When an external voltage of 0.5 V was applied on the membrane, the phenol removal was increased to about 35%. At pH of 6, the phenol was negatively charged with the zeta potential of -3.56 mV. Hence, the enhanced performance of PCM at 0.5 V was mainly caused by the electrically enhanced adsorption. Notably, with the increase of the voltage from +0.5 to +2.0 V, the removal increased dramatically from 35% to 90%. It was noted the enhancement of phenol removal from +0.5 to +1.0 V was more significant than that from 0 to +0.5 V, meaning
a different removal mechanism occurred at the voltage higher than \(+0.5\;\mathrm{V}\). To explore the possible mechanism, the cyclic voltammetry measurement was conducted to investigate the electrochemical behavior towards phenol. Fig. 5b shows an oxidation peak \((+1.0\;\mathrm{V})\) with the onset potential starting from \(+0.8\;\mathrm{V}\) in phenol solution, while no oxidation peak occurs in the solution without phenol. This result verifies that phenol can be electro-oxidized at the potential more than \(+0.8\;\mathrm{V}\) on the surface of membrane. Consequently, the phenol removal was significantly enhanced at the voltage higher than \(+0.5\;\mathrm{V}\).
Typically, the electrolyte concentration is one important factor associated with the electrochemical performance. In this work, the \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) concentration was increased from 10 to \(30\;\mathrm{mM}\), and its effect on performance of electrochemically assisted PCM was investigated. For membrane filtration alone, its phenol removal remains almost unchanged regardless of the electrolyte concentration (Fig. 5c). Evidently, the phenol removal by PCM at \(+0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) reached to \(45\%\), which is higher than that of PCM operated in \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) \((35\%)\). Furthermore, the removal was greatly enhanced to about \(85\%\) at \(+1.0\;\mathrm{V}\), which was much higher than that operated in \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\). These results demonstrate that the increase of electrolyte concentration could enhance the membrane performance under electrochemical assistance, which is owing to the enhanced electrical conductivity in solution. Unexpectedly, further increase of the voltage to \(+1.5\;\mathrm{V}\) results in the slight decrease of phenol removal. According to previous reports [39], the electrochemical behavior could induce the electrolyte precipitation on membrane, which may covers its active sites and results in the decayed performance. As is well known, the high-saline wastewater, which contains various refractory organic pollutants in the presence of high saline concentration, are difficult to be treated by traditional physical, chemical or biological method. The superior performance of electrochemically assisted PCM in at high saline concentration provides a fascinating strategy for the treatment of high-saline wastewater.
Fig. 5. (a) Phenol removal by PCM under different voltage supply in pure water containing \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) ([phenol]: \(10\;\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\); \(\left[\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\right]\): \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\); flow velocity: \(1.0\;\mathrm{mL\cdot min^{-1}}\), transmembrane pressure: 0.2 bar); (b) cyclic voltammetry curves of PCM in \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) with and without phenol. (c) phenol removal by PCM under different voltage supply in simulated seawater ([phenol]: \(10\;\mathrm{mg\cdot L^{-1}}\); \(\left[\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\right]\): \(30\;\mathrm{mM}\); flow velocity: \(1.0\;\mathrm{mL\cdot min^{-1}}\), transmembrane pressure: 0.2 bar).
MO, as a typical anionic dye [40], was also selected to evaluate the capability of PCM for dyes removal under electrochemical assistance. As shown in Fig. 6a, the PCM filtration alone could remove less than \(30\%\) MO within \(60\;\mathrm{min}\) operation. Similar to phenol removal, the removal was mainly attributed to the adsorption of MO molecules on membrane. At \(+0.5\;\mathrm{V}\), the MO removal was increased slightly due to the electrostatic attraction between negatively charged MO and positively charged membrane. It was noted that the MO removal increased dramatically to almost \(100\%\) when the \(+1.0\;\mathrm{V}\) was applied, and the removal was stable at about \(100\%\) as the voltage was further increased to \(+1.5\;\mathrm{V}\). This significant enhancement implies that the electrochemical oxidation of MO occurs when the voltage exceeds the \(+0.5\;\mathrm{V}\). To confirm this
explanation, the cyclic voltammetry curve of PCM was performed from \(-0.5\;\mathrm{V}\) to \(+1.5\) V (Fig. 6b). It could be observed that the oxidation peak appears at the onset potential of \(0.6\;\mathrm{V}\), which demonstrates the MO could be oxidized at potential higher than \(0.6\;\mathrm{V}\) on the membrane.
The reusability of PCM under electrochemical assistance was evaluated through testing its performance for MO removal in five repeated use. As shown in Fig. S7, almost \(100\%\) MO removal could be maintained after 5 consecutive cycles, demonstrating the good stability of electrochemically assisted PCM for water treatment.
## 3.5. Surface water treatment
Based on the above results, integrating PCM with electrochemical function exhibited improved membrane performance and low energy demand (small voltage supply and low driven-pressure), making it a promising strategy for advanced water treatment. To evaluate the performance of electrochemically assisted PCM for real water treatment, the surface water sample was collected from a reservoir near Dalian city. The water quality parameters of the collected water are listed in the Table S4. The membrane filtration process was continuously operated for \(240\;\mathrm{min}\). As shown in Fig. 7, it was clear that the membrane filtration under \(+1.5\;\mathrm{V}\) displayed the better
performance in removing the \(\mathrm{UV}_{254}\), TOC, bacterial and turbidity than that without voltage supply (Fig. 7a). Both the bacteria and turbidity were almost completely eliminated, while the removal efficiency for \(\mathrm{UV}_{254}\) and TOC achieved about \(95\%\) and \(80\%\), respectively. The results indicate that the electrochemically assisted PCM is able to realize effective treatment of surface water. Moreover, the permeate fluxes of the two operation modes were also evaluated (Fig. 7b). The permeate flux of membrane filtration alone dropped dramatically and kept stable at about \(40\;\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\). Under \(+1.5\) V, the permeate flux of PCM displayed obvious increase, which reached stable at about \(90\;\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\). According to previous reports [10, 17], the natural organic matters such as humic acid (HA) and bacteria in surface water are negatively charged. Hence, the electrostatic attraction between HA or bacteria and positively charged membrane inevitably causes aggravated membrane fouling and flux decline. However in this work, the flux loss was greatly inhibited for the electrochemically assisted PCM, which may be attributed to the electrochemical decomposition of HA and bacteria retained on membrane.
The energy consumption in membrane filtration was an important factor to be considered in real water treatment. In electrochemically assisted PCM process, the energy consumption mainly arises from two parts: the pressure-driven consumption and the electrical consumption. Commonly, the energy cost in conventional pressure-driven membrane filtration in surface water treatment was approximately \(30\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{m}^{-3}\) [41]. The electrical consumption was calculated based on the equations (1) and (2), in which I, V, t, J and A referred to current, voltage, time, flux and membrane surface area, respectively. An additional electrical consumption calculated to be \(2\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{m}^{-3}\) (6% of the total energy consumption) was required in the electrochemically assisted PCM process. Despite this, the electrochemically assisted PCM process saved \(30\%\) energy cost due to the improved membrane flux (30%) under electrochemical assistance, as was revealed in Fig. 7b. The net energy consumption in electrochemically assisted PCM process is much less than that of conventional one.
