3.6. Mechanism for the enhanced performance of electrochemically assisted PCM
According to the membrane structure (volume of porous carbon layer \((0.04\;\mathrm{cm}^{3})\)) and operation condition (the flow rate \((1.0\;\mathrm{mL\cdot min^{-1}})\)), the contact time between pollutants and porous carbon layer of PCM was only 2.4 s. Such a short retention time gives rise to the high pollutants removal, which might arise from the efficient mass transfer and plentiful electro-active surface sites on PCM, owing to its unique structure feature. To highlight the high-performance of electrochemically assisted PCM, its efficiency was also compared with other reported porous carbon membrane. As shown in Table S5, the performance of PCM under electrochemical assistance was comparable to other reported porous carbon membranes in water treatment, whereas much lower driven-pressure (only 0.2 bar) was required and higher permeability was achieved in this process.
To unveil the mechanism for the enhanced performance of electrochemically assisted PCM, the electro-active surface area (EASA) of PCM and mass transport rate constant were characterized and calculated. As comparison, the electro-active surface area and mass transport rate constant of CNT- and rGO- membranes (previously reported reactive electrochemical membrane) were also characterized. The EASA of membrane can be derived according to eq. 3 and eq. 4 [42, 43], where the \(\Delta\mathrm{I}\), v and \(\mathrm{C_{d}}\)
refers to the difference of capacitance current, scan rate \((0.1\;\mathrm{V}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) and the double layer capacitance, respectively. As shown in Fig. 8, the PCM exhibited obviously higher \(\Delta\mathrm{I}\) \((1.74\;\mathrm{mA})\) than that of CNT membrane \((0.64\;\mathrm{mA})\) and rGO membrane \((1.28\;\mathrm{mA})\). The EASA of PCM, CNT membrane and rGO membrane were calculated to be 1750, 642 and \(1250\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), respectively. The large electro-active surface area of PCM enables it to effectively adsorb and react with pollutants under electrochemical assistance, which is owing to the high surface area of the porous carbon layer derived from MOFs.
\[
\Delta\mathrm{I}=2\cdot\mathrm{v}\cdot\mathrm{C_d}
\]
