The mass transfer rate constant \(\left(\mathrm{k}_{\mathrm{m}}\right)\) was calculated based on the limited current method (eq. 3) [42, 43] using \(\mathrm{K}_{4}\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\) as the probe, where the n \((\mathfrak{n}{=}1)\), A (11.34 \(\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)), F \((96485\;\mathrm{C\;mol^{-1}})\), and C \((5\;\mathrm{mM})\) correspond to the number of electrons transferred, the geometric surface area of the electrode, the Faraday constant, and the
concentration of the \(\mathrm{K_{4}Fe(CN)_{6}}\) redox species (5 mM), respectively. As depicted in Fig. 9, an obvious oxidation peak at \(\sim1.7\;\mathrm{V}\) was observed for PCM, CNT membrane and rGO membrane, owing to the oxidation of \(\mathrm{K_{4}Fe(CN)_{6}}\) on these membrane electrodes. Distinctively, the peak current followed the order of \(\mathrm{PCM}>\mathrm{CNT}\) membrane \(>\) rGO membrane. Based on eq. 5, the \(\mathrm{k_{m}}\) of porous carbon, CNT membrane and rGO membrane are \(3.1 \times 10^{-5}\), \(2.3 \times 10^{-5}\) and \(1.1 \times 10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\), respectively. The larger \(\mathrm{k_{m}}\) of PCM allows easier transport of the reactants towards the accessible surface reactive sites of PCM, which should thank to the highly porous structure of the MOFs-derived porous carbon layer. Due to the large electro-active surface area and enhanced mass transfer rate, PCM serves as an ideal candidate for electrochemical water treatment.
\[
\mathrm{k_m}=\mathrm{I_{lim}}/(\mathrm{n\cdot F\cdot A\cdot C})
