The electrochemically assisted PCM process was proposed in Fig. 10, when the PCM was supplied with external voltage, three kinds of electrochemical behavior, such as the electrostatic attraction, electrostatic repulsion, and electro-oxidation, were involved in the pollutant removal. For example, if the large particles carry the same kind of charge as the membrane, the electrostatic repulsion between membrane and particle could drive the large particles away from the membrane surface, which not only enhances its removal, but also alleviates the membrane fouling caused by it. For small-
sized organic molecules (e.g. phenol) which are hard to be removed by size-exclusion, the electrostatic attraction provides a feasible method to remove them by attracting negatively charged molecules towards the membrane surface. Subsequently, the electro-oxidation may take place to oxidize or even mineralize the adsorbed pollutants into nonhazardous \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). Considering only small voltage supply \((<1.5\;\mathrm{V})\) was required in the electrochemically assisted PCM process, it is an energy-efficient and promising membrane-based technology for enhanced water treatment.
## 4. Conclusions
A novel porous carbon membrane (PCM), possessing large surface area, superior permeability and good conductivity, was constructed by coating porous carbon derived from MOF-5 on a ceramic membrane substrate. Under negative voltage supply, the PCM displayed both enhanced rejection towards PS microsphere and reduced membrane fouling, owing to the electrostatic repulsion between membrane and microsphere. While under positive voltage supply, the PCM could effectively remove the organic chemicals (unavailable by size-exclusion) with the assistance of electrically enhanced adsorption and electro-oxidation. More importantly, the electrochemical assisted PCM showed enhanced membrane performance and good stability in surface water treatment. The enhanced performance of PCM was mainly attributed to its large electro-active surface area and enhanced mass transfer rate. This work is expected to
provide insight into developing novel electrochemical membrane-based technology for water treatment.
